3. Data Flow
Data can flow in three ways.

A. Simplex
Definition
Data moves in only one direction.

Example

TV Broadcast

TV Station
      ↓
 Television
The TV receives signals but cannot send data back.

B. Half Duplex

Definition
Both devices can send and receive data, but not at the same time.

Example
Walkie-Talkie
Person A → Person B

(Wait)

Person B → Person A

Only one person talks at a time.

C. Full Duplex
Definition
Both devices can send and receive data simultaneously.

Example
Phone Call

Both people can speak and hear at the same time.

Easy Trick
Mode	Communication
Simplex	One Way
Half Duplex	Two Way, One at a Time
Full Duplex	Two Way, Same Time



⭐ Interview Answer
Simplex allows one-way communication, Half Duplex allows two-way communication but only one device transmits at a time, and Full Duplex allows both devices to communicate simultaneously.

Interview Questions
Explain Simplex, Half Duplex, and Full Duplex.
Give one example of each.



4. Bandwidth
Definition
Bandwidth is the maximum amount of data that can be transmitted through a network in one second.

Unit
bps (bits per second)
Mbps
Gbps
Example

A 100 Mbps internet connection means the network can transfer up to 100 million bits per second under ideal conditions.

Bandwidth is the capacity of the network.
⭐ Interview Answer
Bandwidth is the maximum capacity of a network to transfer data in a given amount of time.

Interview Questions
What is Bandwidth?
What is the unit of Bandwidth?

5. Throughput
Definition
Throughput is the actual amount of data successfully transferred over the network.

Example
Suppose your internet plan is 100 Mbps, but because of network traffic you actually receive 80 Mbps.

Bandwidth = 100 Mbps
Throughput = 80 Mbps
Easy Analogy
Highway capacity = 6 lanes (Bandwidth)

Traffic due to congestion = Cars move through only 4 lanes effectively (Throughput)

⭐ Interview Answer
Throughput is the actual data transfer rate achieved over a network.

Interview Questions
What is Throughput?
Difference between Bandwidth and Throughput?


6. Bandwidth vs Throughput

Bandwidth  	Throughput
Maximum capacity	Actual speed achieved
Theoretical value	Practical value
Usually higher	Usually lower or equal

7. Latency
Definition
Latency is the time taken by data to travel from the sender to the receiver.

Unit
Milliseconds (ms)

Example
When playing an online game, if your ping is 20 ms, your commands reach the game server in about 20 milliseconds.
Lower latency means faster response.
Easy Analogy

Think of sending a courier.
The time it takes to reach the destination is like latency.
⭐ Interview Answer
Latency is the delay between sending data and receiving it.

Interview Questions
What is Latency?
Why is low latency important for gaming and video calls?


8. Packet
Definition
Large data is divided into small pieces called packets before being sent over a network.

Example
A 100 MB file is broken into many packets.

Each packet travels through the network and is reassembled at the destination.

Why Packetization?
Faster transmission
Easier error recovery
Efficient use of the network
Interview Questions
What is a Packet?
Why is data divided into packets?


9. Frame
Definition
A Frame is a packet with additional information (such as source and destination MAC addresses) added by the Data Link Layer for communication within a local network.
Simple way to remember: Packet + Data Link Layer information = Frame

Packet vs Frame
Packet	Frame
Created by Network Layer	Created by Data Link Layer
Uses IP Address	Uses MAC Address



Interview Questions
What is a Frame?
Difference between Packet and Frame?
